      "problem": "If $p^2 + 27 = 0,$ then $p =$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\np^2 + 27 & = 0\\[3pt]\n\np^2 & = -27 \n}\n$$\n\nBy taking the square root, we obtain\n\n$$\np = \\pm \\sqrt{-27}.\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $p$ using the imaginary unit $\\textrm{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\, \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =\\pm \\sqrt{-27} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{27\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{27}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}\\cdot 3}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 3\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 3\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $p = \\pm 3\\sqrt{3} \\, \\textrm{i}.$",
